---
/**
 * The mechanism behind one row of attention: what every word makes, and what
 * happens when one word's question meets everybody's label.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT WHAT THE INSTRUMENT SHOWS. `AttentionMap` shows the weights
 * and the meaning that comes out; it never shows where the numbers came from,
 * because nine words times three tags is more than one panel can hold. So the
 * diagram takes the structure — a question, a label, contents — and the budget
 * bar, and the instrument takes the behaviour.
 *
 * The teaching load is carried by the WIDTHS of the three segments and by the
 * printed percentages beside them, never by tone: the bar reads identically in
 * greyscale, and every segment names itself (hard rule 9). The bracket
 * underneath is the point of the whole picture — the segments span exactly the
 * bar, no more and no less, whichever word is asking.
 *
 * Every number here was sampled from the same arithmetic `AttentionMap` runs,
 * for "she" under the question "who do I stand for?" — 43%, 16%, and about 6%
 * to each of the other seven. Those figures are pinned by that instrument's
 * tests, which fail if the shared sentence ever drifts away from them.
 *
 * Decorative on its own, so `aria-hidden`: `Figure` carries the accessible name
 * and the description.
 */
---

<svg viewBox="0 0 660 348" class="w-full" aria-hidden="true">
  <g class="fill-ink font-display" font-size="13" font-weight="700">
    <text x="16" y="24">Every word turns itself into three things</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-none stroke-rule-strong" stroke-width="1.25">
    <rect x="16" y="38" width="284" height="122" rx="8"></rect>
    <rect x="360" y="38" width="284" height="122" rx="8"></rect>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-paper-sunken">
    <rect x="30" y="50" width="52" height="21" rx="4"></rect>
    <rect x="374" y="50" width="72" height="21" rx="4"></rect>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink font-mono" font-size="11">
    <text x="40" y="65">she</text>
    <text x="384" y="65">student</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9">
    <text x="30" y="94">asks</text>
    <text x="30" y="118">offers</text>
    <text x="30" y="142">hands over</text>
    <text x="374" y="94">asks</text>
    <text x="374" y="118">offers</text>
    <text x="374" y="142">hands over</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted" font-size="10">
    <text x="112" y="94">who am I standing for?</text>
    <text x="112" y="118">I am a pointing word</text>
    <text x="112" y="142">very little on its own</text>
    <text x="456" y="94">saved for its own turn</text>
    <text x="456" y="118">I am a person</text>
    <text x="456" y="142">everything “student” means</text>
  </g>

  <!-- The one comparison this row is built on: she's question against
       student's label. Drawn with its own head so there is no shared id. -->
  <path
    class="fill-none stroke-accent"
    stroke-width="1.5"
    d="M302 90 C 322 90, 336 114, 352 114"></path>
  <polygon class="fill-accent" points="352,110 360,114 352,118"></polygon>

  <g class="fill-accent font-mono" font-size="9">
    <text x="304" y="80">match</text>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9">
    <text x="16" y="180"
      >every one of the nine words makes all three, and every question is put to
      every label</text
    >
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink font-display" font-size="13" font-weight="700">
    <text x="16" y="212"
      >The matches become shares, and the shares add to one</text
    >
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-muted" font-size="10">
    <text x="16" y="230"
      >what “she” spends its one unit of attention on, under that question</text
    >
  </g>

  <!-- The budget. Segment widths ARE the weights; the labels underneath say
       which is which, so nothing depends on telling the three tones apart. -->
  <g class="stroke-rule-strong" stroke-width="1">
    <rect x="16" y="240" width="271.4" height="26" class="fill-accent-2"></rect>
    <rect x="287.4" y="240" width="103.4" height="26" class="fill-accent-2-soft"
    ></rect>
    <rect x="390.8" y="240" width="253.2" height="26" class="fill-paper-sunken"
    ></rect>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink font-mono" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="151.7" y="284">student — 43%</text>
    <text x="339.1" y="284">box — 16%</text>
    <text x="517.4" y="284">the other seven words — about 6% each</text>
  </g>

  <!-- The bracket is the claim: the segments span the bar exactly. -->
  <g class="stroke-rule-strong" stroke-width="1">
    <line x1="16" y1="296" x2="644" y2="296"></line>
    <line x1="16" y1="292" x2="16" y2="300"></line>
    <line x1="644" y1="292" x2="644" y2="300"></line>
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink-faint font-mono" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">
    <text x="330" y="312">one whole unit — the same for every word, always</text
    >
  </g>

  <g class="fill-ink font-display" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
    <text x="16" y="336"
      >so “she” leaves meaning 0.43 a person, 0.29 a thing, 0.06 a pointing word</text
    >
  </g>
</svg>
